the_loop:
Nearly every method in this explainer runs some version of this loop, or part of it.

Step 0, start. Begin from a pretrained policy that already succeeds sometimes: a BC diffusion or flow policy, ACT, or a VLA such as pi0, pi0.5 or OpenVLA-OFT. If it never succeeds (0%), most methods have nothing to climb: on five RoboTwin 2.0 tasks, SimpleVLA-RL on an OpenVLA-OFT base model given no task demonstrations (0-trajectory SFT) stays at 0% after RL, because every rollout earns zero reward. The exceptions change how they explore instead: PDE has a VLM rewrite the instruction and climbs LIBERO-PRO from a 0% start.

Step 1, deploy. Run the policy to collect episodes. They can come from the real robot, a fleet, a GPU simulator, a scanned or generated digital twin (the sim-to-real family), or a learned action-conditioned video world model (the world-model family). Each has a cost: real episodes need resets and supervision, and simulated ones can be wrong in ways the policy will exploit.

Step 2, measure. Turn each episode into a number. The options are a binary success check (a human keypress, a hand-written detector, a classifier), a dense progress or value model (the reward family), or a VLM judge. For comparing checkpoints, you want an evaluator that ranks policies the same way reality does, reported as Pearson r and MMRV against paired real trials (the world-model/evaluation family). Offline validation action MSE is a poor proxy: when SIMPLER ranked 6 checkpoints on 3 Google Robot tasks, MSE averaged Pearson r 0.308 against real success, vs 0.924 for visual-matching sim.

Step 3, find failures. Locate where and why the policy goes wrong. The tools are dips in a progress model (Dyna), runtime monitors, either calibrated on successful runs (CoRe) or triggered when the critic's Q jumps away from its recent moving average (JITI, which targets lapses in execution quality rather than outright failures), human takeovers (the HITL family), VLM diagnosis of failed videos (F4R, PDE), and per-stage predicates (Temporal GRPO). This step decides where the next batch of data, search or corrections goes.

Step 4, improve. Apply an update. Each family is a different update operator:
- black-box search perturbs a small parameter set (gains, a noise vector, a recipe) and keeps what scores better;
- on-policy PPO/GRPO raises the log-probability of rollouts that beat a baseline;
- off-policy actor-critic learns Q from every stored transition and pushes actions toward higher Q;
- residual, edit and steering methods train a small add-on around a frozen base;
- advantage-weighted or advantage-conditioned BC keeps the supervised loss but chooses what to copy;
- DAgger-style methods train on human (or machine) corrections at the states the policy actually reaches;
- test-time search leaves the policy's weights unchanged: it samples candidates and picks with a verifier, or guides the sampler with a critic's gradient (the verifier or critic may itself be trained).

Step 5, re-measure. Evaluate on held-out episodes and start states that were not used for search or training. Report trial counts and confidence intervals, and track speed, intervention rate and per-task results, not just average success. Then repeat. Many loops also distill what worked back into the base (PLD, VERITAS); Q-Planning instead refits only its critic each round and leaves the policy frozen. Others restart each round from the pretrained checkpoint to avoid drift (RECAP). Restarting does not fix a skewed data mix, though: DEED, a RECAP adaptation on a humanoid, restarted every round and still fell from 42% (21/50) to 22% (11/50) in round 2 (its round-1 gain over the 32% starting policy was not statistically conclusive); its authors attribute the drop to self-generated rollouts crowding out the teleoperation demos.

How the other families plug in:
- reward and value models serve steps 2-3 for everyone else;
- world models and twins make steps 1-2 cheap;
- HITL methods put a person into steps 3-4;
- industry recipes are mostly this loop built as infrastructure: fleet logging, auto-grading, targeted retraining.
